  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring the lottery with the lower probability of the maximum outcome. In this case, Lottery A has 80% (lower) vs. Lottery B's 100% (higher), so the reason would favor A. The individual does consider the probability of the maximum outcome of each lottery, especially focusing on the 80% chance for 4000 Euros and the certainty of 3000 Euros. However, the individual's justification is driven more by the attractiveness of the higher payoff and a dismissal of the 20% failure chance, rather than a deliberate preference for the lottery with the lower probability of its maximum outcome. The match in considered information is partial, but the rule-based logic of the reason (prefer lower probability of max outcome) is not explicitly or implicitly mirrored in the verbal justification. The individual seems to prefer A despite its lower probability of success, not because of it. Therefore, while some relevant information is considered, the reasoning process does not align with the rule of the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason focuses on the probability of the maximum outcome of each lottery. For Lottery A, the maximum outcome is 4000 Euros with a probability of 20%. For Lottery B, the maximum outcome is 3000 Euros with a probability of 25%. The reason prefers the lottery with the lower probability of the maximum outcome. Comparing the two, Lottery A has a 20% chance (lower) of yielding its maximum outcome, while Lottery B has a 25% chance (higher). Therefore, according to the decision reason, Lottery A would be preferred because it has the lower probability (20% < 25%) of the maximum outcome. The relevant information (maximum outcomes and their probabilities) is available in the decision problem, so the reason can be applied.",
